          "ans": "Osmosis is the net diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane down its water potential gradient:\n\n1. Hypotonic Solution (Lower solute concentration / Higher water potential):\n• Plant Cell: Water enters by endosmosis into the central vacuole. The protoplast swells and presses firmly against the rigid cellulose wall, generating Turgor Pressure. The cell becomes firm and turgid; it does not burst.\n• Animal Cell: Water rushes in continuously. Lacking a cell wall, the fragile plasma membrane stretches and ruptures (Lysis / Hemolysis in RBCs).\n\n2. Hypertonic Solution (Higher solute concentration / Lower water potential):\n• Plant Cell: Water exits by exosmosis. The central vacuole shrinks and the plasma membrane pulls away from the rigid cell wall in the process of Plasmolysis. The tissue wilts.\n• Animal Cell: Water leaves the cytoplasm. The cell shrinks, shrivels, and forms notched irregular edges (Crenation).\n\n3. Isotonic Solution (Equal solute concentration / Balanced water potential):\n• Plant Cell: Equal inward and outward water movement. No net turgor develops; cells become flaccid, causing herbaceous plants to droop.\n• Animal Cell: Dynamic equilibrium; cell maintains normal spherical/biconcave shape and optimal osmotic balance."
