          "q": "Detail the chromosomal behavior and sub-stages of Prophase-I of Meiosis. Explain Synapsis, Tetrad formation, Chiasmata, and Crossing Over.",
          "cognitiveLevel": "Understanding",
          "ans": "Prophase-I is the critical engine of meiosis divided into five dynamic stages:\n\n1. Leptotene:\n• Chromatin condenses into long, thin, bead-like chromosomes with chromomeres.\n\n2. Zygotene (Synapsis):\n• Homologous maternal and paternal chromosomes recognize each other and pair precisely gene-for-gene.\n• This pairing process is called Synapsis, stabilized by a protein complex called the synaptonemal complex.\n\n3. Pachytene (Crossing Over):\n• Chromosomes thicken further. Each bivalent now visibly consists of four chromatids, termed a Tetrad.\n• Non-sister chromatids intertwine at specific contact points called Chiasmata.\n• Crossing Over occurs: endonucleases break non-sister chromatids, homologous DNA segments are reciprocally exchanged, and ligases seal the recombinant chromatids.\n\n4. Diplotene:\n• Synaptonemal complex dissolves, and homologous chromosomes begin to repel each other but remain tightly anchored at chiasmata.\n\n5. Diakinesis:\n• Chiasmata shift toward the ends of chromosomes in terminalization.\n• Nucleolus vanishes, nuclear envelope disassembles, and spindle apparatus forms to usher the tetrads into Metaphase-I."
