          "cognitiveLevel": "Understanding",
          "ans": "Mechanism of Catalysis via Induced Fit:\n\n1. Initial Recognition & Binding:\n• The substrate approaches the enzyme's active site via random thermal diffusion.\n• Weak non-covalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, electrostatic attractions, hydrophobic forces) begin forming between substrate functional groups and amino acid residues in the binding site.\n\n2. Induced Conformational Change:\n• Substrate binding induces a conformational shift in the enzyme's protein backbone, closing the active site snugly around the substrate (analogous to a hand closing around a ball).\n\n3. Transition State Stabilization & Bond Strain:\n• The active site places precise mechanical and electrostatic strain on specific covalent bonds within the substrate molecule.\n• Acidic or basic residues donate or accept protons, stabilizing the high-energy Transition State.\n• This dramatic stabilization lowers the activation energy barrier (Ea).\n\n4. Chemical Conversion:\n• Strained bonds break, new covalent bonds form, and the substrate is transformed into the product molecules.\n\n5. Product Dissociation:\n• The products have a different chemical geometry and charge distribution, losing affinity for the active site.\n• Products diffuse out into the medium, and the enzyme springs back to its resting conformation, ready to repeat the cycle."
